    {
      "modify_from": "For an unsampled turn, fit the incoming and outgoing planar path directions from tip positions on their respective legs, using multiple frames wherever available. Extend those fitted segments to their intersection to recover the shared endpoint; with noisy observations, use a joint least-squares fit with a shared endpoint.",
      "modify_to": "Before fitting an unsampled turn, assign each measurement to its chronological leg. Use the preceding arrival and samples strictly before the turn for the incoming segment, and samples strictly after the turn and the following arrival for the outgoing segment. Do not treat a departure sample as evidence of the preceding arrival. Fit the incoming and outgoing planar directions using multiple frames wherever available, preferentially using clear interior distal-tip measurements; downweight uncertain measurements and exclude occluded-tip substitutions. Fit both world x and y freely: an apparently axial image trajectory or vertical shaft does not justify preserving a current world coordinate. Extend both fitted segments to their intersection to recover the shared endpoint; with noisy observations, use a joint least-squares fit with a shared endpoint."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Project the recovered endpoint back into the demonstration to check consistency with both segments and the demonstrated obstacle side.",
      "modify_to": "Project the recovered endpoint and both fitted segments back into the demonstration. Check that the assigned distal-tip observations lie on their respective segments in chronological order, that the endpoint follows the last incoming observation and precedes the first outgoing observation along the route, and that the demonstrated obstacle side is preserved. Reject an endpoint supported by extending only one fitted direction, misassigned arrival/departure evidence, or a rounded trail corner treated as a straight tip trajectory. If directions remain underconstrained, use adjoining arrival evidence and other demonstrated views of the same geometric location; do not invent a longer clearance move."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "The retained starting view or a clearly observed visit to the same gap can supply an additional localization constraint without merging separate route occurrences.",
      "modify_to": "Gather arrival and departure evidence from all demonstrated visits to the same static gap to constrain its common location, including the retained starting view and any clear final visit. A still-approaching sample constrains the incoming segment, not the arrival position. Share the gap location without merging chronological occurrences: each visit consumes its own leg. Do not impose shared coordinates on bypass turns merely because they pass the same obstacle end."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "For the final endpoint, prioritize the actual lower tip in the final demonstration frame, corroborated by preceding arrival frames, and project it onto z=0.07 m. Do not extrapolate beyond it to a nominal gap coordinate or nominal leg length.",
      "modify_to": "For the final endpoint, explicitly identify the distal-tip centre in the final demonstration frame and corroborate the feature identification with preceding arrival frames. Apply the calibrated z=0.07 m ray-plane calculation to that pixel and reproject the resulting coordinate to check numerical consistency; reprojection alone does not establish that the chosen feature is the tip. Discard any pixel/world-coordinate pair that disagrees under the calibration and resolve the measurement rather than accepting either as authoritative. If the final tip is occluded, use the existing occlusion and arrival-evidence rules, not a shaft or trail substitute. Do not extrapolate beyond the supported stopping point to a nominal gap coordinate or nominal leg length."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "No memory fields or transitions change: the reported history and completed-leg updates were correct. No route-specific coordinates, frame-specific exceptions, execution-waypoint-derived endpoint memory, literal pixel-coordinate examples, extra subdivisions, or extra clearance are added. The previous round's geometric reconstruction rules remain; the report identifies evidence-assignment and feature-localization errors, not evidence that those rules should be reverted. Sparse or occluded views are not assumed to uniquely determine an endpoint, and an explicit final-frame tip measurement is not required when that feature is genuinely occluded."
